#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135110 :tutorial/content "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,\n$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp\n$$\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp\n$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4"} :step/id #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f"} :step/id #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e"} :step/id #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135111 :tutorial/content "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3cf2fb6734efe387ad41c59743385154d7a172d2cc10ee0fe69ac6a7f2b342a.svg)\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$\n![](images/60/60574ae7f9fc09522e9d725c787dce63228c67e19cbef002b14e29c1f27e4b20.svg)\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5454 :tutorial/title "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose"} :step/id #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135112 :tutorial/content "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.\n$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5455 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135113 :tutorial/content "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5456 :tutorial/title "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0"}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134041} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248} :activity/id #uuid "d12b0c27-35c4-517e-8eb3-41dab352b8fa" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246 :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498"} :step/id #uuid "453b68d4-1d9d-50c9-a90c-51c8b99946a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245 :question/id #uuid "b9c49e80-5070-56aa-b070-99b1fa2fdff3"} :step/id #uuid "6ef8f97d-5b2a-5ab0-998f-92729ef9dc31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407243 :question/id #uuid "5d75070f-88dd-5b20-8fec-c1f3c6de013c"} :step/id #uuid "6f121dc0-6e9f-506e-8370-6a73e7b21516" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244 :question/id #uuid "66ca173a-d952-58d1-b484-502590c17693"} :step/id #uuid "9114f2aa-68cd-5878-a5bf-ad32cd59fd29"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247 :question/id #uuid "de769de3-3ab7-5536-ae28-2dcbd744b48f"} :step/id #uuid "d8a9f51b-41b7-5b08-9832-d19d696012d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251}}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407212} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495175} :activity/id #uuid "9ad0665c-4138-59ae-93cc-e6c2dc9739c7" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134070 :question/id #uuid "1f3188e0-