d3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d09262-d89e-5778-ba27-e28913c84b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470859} {:answer/id #uuid "ed40d0fb-ff50-5e6b-9b1f-0ac9cefa339b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470859} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ee920f-0442-5db6-b451-a0b35c211448" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de94ea6a-cbe4-5a33-bd3a-207b5830d7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470861} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6f6f29-d475-51a5-96bb-6920805c5262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470862} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5227833-d2bb-5cdd-839d-430c5143ad5d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2265464f-039e-5530-b971-1d47b26a93f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470863} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f59f7d-356e-56e0-803d-4229a968217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470863} :answer-field/id #uuid "75f3ae48-a10b-5299-b200-d844511e2a81" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471937}] :question/id #uuid "fd0235ca-04db-545b-b3a6-a1ff38871984" :question/math-academy-id "q-109853" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(B)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ is the left null space of $B.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${B}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$, that is, each column of $B$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e37cdb6-c2f8-5f2d-b68e-1ab7dae2c068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ee36b-6930-5033-bbff-c6e5831b6de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} :answer-field/id #uuid "48015e6d-6aef-53c6-aa13-f63167aef2d9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c132e320-a78b-5003-a32b-1274e70dff6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472504} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9107f0-649f-5e5e-8c94-d1089de849cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff283957-040e-592b-8540-faef6177d2d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c7e552-c7ec-5bd5-9721-98ed03dbe1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} {:answer/id #uuid "bce36cf7-e320-5e6d-97a5-94061b6e55fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2b47b3-71ab-5d28-ac5e-96cdddfab7f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473648}] :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18841c85-4169-5e21-aa60-7f9429a69ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476243} {:answer/id #uuid "32eb844c-ce40-54f8-8182-8bdb52ef41a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476316} {:answer/id #uuid "be8b7e98-b032-5486-9065-1ba9e55cd64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476636} {:answer/id #uuid "c719959b-438d-5216-87e0-1e590c4937dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476652} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f9aeac-42a7-57b9-961a-8f9b80d6c9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476316} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef854973-f40e-54d0-8398-b5ed45b6afdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476861}] :question/id #uuid "3930016a-7426-5deb-b0f0-c2074d3a1ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109856" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ for all compatible matrices $A$ and $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A+B)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $A+B,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $A+B,$ we actually multiply each row of $A+B$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $A+B,$ which is $\\text{Col}((A+B)^{T})=\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6d4e70-0448-5ab5-9f2d-ce038f339c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476265} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd9368f-9feb-504f-a71d-cd5bb34f56ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476479} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6f1a68-607c-5b44-a425-625c20d52a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476589} {:answer/id #uuid "e41a1e37-3b17-5fc8-8508-7b738b3b3074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476710} {:answer/id #uuid "f85e9333-5d0e-542b-837b-a916ca56e2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476589} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9bb4ab-702b-5516-8021-d3e236ee68e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476778}] :question/id #uuid "4d68ce86-72b3-53d7-b424-c8c3717fc180" :question/math-academy-id "q-109857" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(YX)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(YX)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T}$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the row space of ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ which is $\\text{Col}(({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})^{T})=\\text{Col}(YX).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ab1b2f-ec6e-507b-9701-7686776d5ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513728} {:answer/id #uuid "4b89e055-2ad7-5945-ba06-41fd9a844ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513736} {:answer/id #uuid "5279f1a0-7964-5e07-bb5f-5a1cc2249ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513759} {:answer/id #uuid "a13c9b82-7a72-5392-b4b2-9e4a90902573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513947} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1d87ab-3668-53ac-951d-faeb38987ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514112} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9559f3-a3c8-59ae-9017-a04ed616a025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514336}] :question/id #uuid "9d632f6e-3556-5d97-834c-4e1de9c56681" :question/math-academy-id "q-109858" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ for all compatible matrices $A$ and $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A+B)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A+B)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ is the row space of $({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})^{T}=A+B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A+B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A+B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $A+B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A+B,$ we actually multiply each row of $A+B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A+B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(A+B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48b52c8d-5a6d-57b6-b139-de7e62153564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565793} {:answer/id #uuid "768b47d4-ad9c-5a4c-818c-2d77de8a37b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565865} {:answer/id #uuid "7aced2dd-2260-5a76-91a1-48cb49a84605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565871} {:answer/id #uuid "c36cab80-11fb-53dd-8c1f-1830080636ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565990} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4475e7-9df7-5f01-8da5-2b4f45ddcbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565871} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c992c9-8052-5ffe-8e34-a8bb4e14e451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566119}] :question/id #uuid "f11ffdfd-619b-56d9-be79-9ad5a11a6df9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109851" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(B)$ for every matrix $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(B)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}(B)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $B,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133703 :question/id #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-9099" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = 3,$ then find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)).$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=8,$ $n=6,$ and $n-r=3.$ \n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into $n-r=3,$ we can find the rank of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n6 - r = 3 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nr = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T})) & =r=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8967c8-7a09-5aca-be92-070777e639f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362638} {:answer/id #uuid "ae42776d-9890-5f6d-ba04-a42b0f4d6b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362639} {:answer/id #uuid "14529e7e-b092-5d31-b4ed-2ba06143f782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362640} {:answer/id #uuid "642ddf76-83d6-5526-be68-f3cdea71c79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362641} {:answer/id #uuid "68bf0219-bcf9-574d-aa49-4687d9a8c8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362640} :answer-field/id #uuid "5401f578-6bbd-541f-accf-4f7429d07e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362637}] :question/id #uuid "1f3188e0-a129-5566-aa12-cb0dd9585e31" :question/math-academy-id "q-108790" :question/problem "Consider a $3\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=2,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=3,$ $n=4,$ and $m-r=2.$ \n\nSubstituting $m=3$ into $m-r=2,$ we can find the rank of $A:$\n\n$$\n(3)-r=2\\,⟹\\,r=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) & =r=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1abcc03-91d1-552b-9d4d-9a115b7162b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362738} {:answer/id #uuid "c60509e5-818a-5e5e-b197-6af69774dd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db