:answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394040} {:answer/id #uuid "17e21db7-2740-54eb-87ab-43f4c7023e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394041} {:answer/id #uuid "656d0480-66ba-51ee-b9a6-82fc1fbfd015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394042} {:answer/id #uuid "298f458f-dd8f-56ee-86d2-8a73d3189728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394040} :answer-field/id #uuid "92b7acaf-3ad9-5680-910b-7d4f59738910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394038}] :question/id #uuid "a4dce28d-b322-5c7f-88fd-a0c01e48564e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80671" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y+3z \\\\ 2x+y+z \\\\ x-y+5z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (-2)+3+3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+3+k \\\\ -2-3+5\\cdot k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3k \\\\ -1+k \\\\ -5+5k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3+3k &=0 \\\\ -1+k &=0 \\\\ -5+5k &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19000333-c03d-58cd-bb96-adf67e2fb6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419552} {:answer/id #uuid "244d93d2-8121-51bc-8e96-aa2ac19ce6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419554} {:answer/id #uuid "976c2b45-35ab-56ac-a3e7-15a5ac6660c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419568} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d3097e-28c9-5153-abed-3949a0a1f1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419569} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f206b-887d-51e2-9c05-298e3a299a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419554} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c83c874-69fc-5829-8d75-f28fa19db372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419576}] :question/id #uuid "63f7892f-01e8-58a2-8307-26e6eb1dacb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140051" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-x-2y-2z \\\\ -z \\\\ x+2y+3z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-(-6)-2\\cdot k-2\\cdot 0 \\\\ -0 \\\\ -6+2\\cdot k+3\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6-2k \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6+2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6-2k &=0 \\\\ -6+2k &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to these two equations is $k=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002e3086-b98b-552c-addc-3f03c123250a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419549} {:answer/id #uuid "074dbe6b-ac14-5305-9b21-791a127cd1b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419550} {:answer/id #uuid "54b40f38-942c-56c1-a0a6-462a9318f8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419560} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab3c7ac-b751-5ee8-8682-76fba4b200e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419566} {:answer/id #uuid "e26456a4-5ad0-5391-b8ab-b6896539894e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419571} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c315ae-a887-5ed7-90f8-efa5df25967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419577}] :question/id #uuid "f1fb1c9c-0f88-5161-9527-cc60a53751f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80666" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-2x-14y \\\\ 3x+21y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2\\cdot 7-14\\cdot k \\\\ 3\\cdot 7+21\\cdot k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-14-14k \\\\ 21+21k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-14-14k &=0 \\\\ 21+21k &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d87305-75ca-5b78-b5e8-ae68465f1ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454904} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9b2610-7605-5718-a92d-9dfc87c5b7bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454975} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3e795c-08ca-5b60-839f-c5d60fdc4b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455076} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cdcbf-ef2d-5021-bec3-b5bc3b245dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455102} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2c4637-fa23-5b05-874b-8c92ec63e9f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455102} :answer-field/id #uuid "8043bbfb-4ba7-5390-8582-7ad291ce380a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455376}] :question/id #uuid "d7ba141f-fb4c-5677-9c12-2cecf5acd474" :question/math-academy-id "q-81389" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y \\\\ 15x+5y \\\\ -6x-2y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot k+6 \\\\ 15\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6 \\\\ -6\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3k+6 \\\\ 15k+30 \\\\ -6k-12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3k+6 &=0 \\\\ 15k+30 &=0 \\\\ -6k-12 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124546 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124355 :question/id #uuid "5ba5ded2-bfca-5e53-9056-8aede726505f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5616" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n![](images/0e/0e394f7c76e9f65e5216d627041f6a906ef2e580de85939e2d64026b82ab18ee.svg) \nand the vectors \n![](images/f1/f19b4a1174a7b45c56e02972a93fc4761dcff3dd5830b0ac1866d9f80f3bd86f.svg)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd670b1b-6491-54b8-99cf-231c52763619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123219} {:answer/id #uuid "16fe36c9-340b-5f82-913c-7f61c4421b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123220} {:answer/id #uuid "2416900d-0c68-55bc-9ec8-14a3bd1deaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123221} {:answer/id #uuid "69a56d1a-84da-54af-9478-b268e010a5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123222} {:answer/id #uuid "7783510c-ee1c-5e3d-ab5d-9b0b489ef1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123223}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c296fda-e7ab-550a-8fcc-a683fe276764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123218}] :question/id #uuid "76a36780-30de-5702-9e33-0569cc0c2683" :question/math-academy-id "q-37824" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - y \\\\ -8x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8877df09-2fc0-5dea-ac70-3b79adb6d1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123417} {:answer/id #uuid "91942fbf-fa10-5545-ac72-02441be46a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123418} {:answer/id #uuid "b792c5f4-654c-56e3-b28c-9df75cf32a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123419} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7af9b8-0226-5d14-a9ec-76eac9bfe895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}, v_{2}$, and $v_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123420} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdbaf87-8641-5efa-a322-b4236d905681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123421}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c4e21c2-3e02-53b9-b588-691233f3e416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123416}] :question/id #uuid "79a32dd0-137a-5477-a90d-f7406129b208" :question/math-academy-id "q-38684" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2y + 3z \\\\ 4x + y + z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $Ker(T)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070baad4-ad33-576e-8c10-a318d6e24cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269332} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0e050-f330-5a1f-bcc4-cb34b06b865a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270096} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea1a9bf-b113-5ae1-91d8-81b5bf35f9ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270107} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfb5ce2-72e9-5f83-a46c-2cb7f06d73d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270656} {:answer/id #uuid "e28386a4-5eee-51eb-a73b-54fd86410c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b615d50c-14ad-5847-9c65-aea5729ff569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271842}] :question/id #uuid "14df7021-329f-5b79-9249-773f1affa3e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139998" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x+2y \\\\ 6x+3y\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3542d1a9-0149-5a87-845a-c2a1358d7505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321555} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7dfa2f-34db-56d9-a8fb-ff799a2f2948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321879} {:answer/id #uuid "894064c2-fa57-59f6-a23e-da4997fa484f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322241} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb5a7b2-df0c-52da-ad36-b5e249253134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322737} {:answer/id #uuid "d84d5ce8-ec22-54a6-9429-dcd6dbbd5b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321555} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53b2e36-9418-53bf-a391-31c49bfdb506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323590}] :question/id #uuid "2204189b-4707-5bbc-8475-ce3acaaee8eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37712" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -84\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c9e4a52-aec6-5b65-bcce-57a1d297b44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321616} {:answer/id #uuid "4aefadd2-6834-5b49-9fd0-318aab266db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321737} {:answer/id #uuid "6b95369b-9cc9-5059-9ea6-f418fd6fe7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321986} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8b2894-57d2-5b75-872e-4ef069a1b777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322398} {:answer/id #uuid "f91e37e3-3115-58d7-9287-7a7c29e4881a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321986} :answer-field/id #uuid "17471e30-4b35-5a19-bc16-09cec08480ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323212}] :question/id #uuid "cf0747fe-fc9d-5c59-8014-fc99ebcd5660" :question/math-academy-id "q-37840" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+4z \\\\ -5x+10y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db32447-a4ce-5dff-9487-a82e227e41f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402711} {:answer/id #uuid "8275882b-84f3-5627-93d4-2c259ae73a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402712} {:answer/id #uuid "814b7b1e-69ce-596a-8267-686227a9ea9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402713} {:answer/id #uuid "4a769e20-30bc-5034-8119-ec017476bd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402714} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbec318-84b4-5cc1-a7c5-50ee378933cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402711} :answer-field/id #uuid "17572c2b-682c-5ae4-a962-e180a05850b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402710}] :question/id #uuid "4bfe7ea3-d20b-5a92-b7ac-e6c2ec81d721" :question/math-academy-id "q-40179" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{i})\\ne 0$ for any $i$'s, we conclude that none of them belongs to the kernel of $\\,T.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} {:answer/id #uuid "15868a01-ce63-5e6b-b375-fec48a6e6ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404180} {:answer/id #uuid "55de43a8-0397-50c5-867b-d34348940323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404181} {:answer/id #uuid "5c165536-c143-5156-8063-ecfdc1329b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404182} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1de765-7e1f-51a4-a847-e7a575a67eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404178}] :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34110eb5-9c97-5c56-9869-82bc944c8d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454985} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff79594-e24e-5762-86ab-0aeb3424c662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455009} {:answer/id #uuid "5a74588d-7905-5df0-b42a-f72dc5308f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455060} {:answer/id #uuid "cd32bbc5-2f74-5d0f-9a57-6de98d239eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455257} {:answer/id #uuid "e58c2082-609d-5083-974a-1c5cf03a9e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455257} :answer-field/id #uuid "7714a9fc-9cb2-5973-a0f5-19e9eb492e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455372}] :question/id #uuid "52f90c3e-788d-5e75-8c65-475f701ec88b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81384" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x+3y \\\\ -12x-9y\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4d803c-1e53-5bf2-a8ee-8bddd3cfeb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455029} {:answer/id #uuid "6339c59a-406d-52cf-a948-16ba1dd58aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455081} {:answer/id #uuid "d64d5dc3-1705-5854-b0d1-3a1a2cbb943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455277} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4df685-2392-55ce-b95a-a28b76007ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455314} {:answer/id #uuid "f49987ea-0169-5cab-85eb-ff1ee67fd47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455314} :answer-field/id #uuid "8724cf4c-2634-5ef0-8c70-8b6da229c63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455382}] :question/id #uuid "e5cb0f8a-8e07-5249-bc79-49a7b77ca413" :question/math-academy-id "q-80663" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+2y \\\\ -9x-6y\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1960 :topic/title "The Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}]}
